# p11-kit -- the PKCS#11 module router, and where the CA trust store lives.
#
# Two jobs. It multiplexes PKCS#11 modules (smartcards, software tokens) behind
# one interface, and it ships the TRUST MODULE, which is what turns a pile of
# CA certificates into something GnuTLS can query. gcr and gnome-keyring reach
# tokens through it; GnuTLS reaches the system trust store through it.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
# BLFS 12.4 pins 0.25.5.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/p11-glue/p11-kit/releases/download/0.25.5/p11-kit-0.25.5.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=04d0a86450cdb1be018f26af6699857171a188ac6d5b8c90786a60854e1198e5
SRC_DIR=p11-kit-0.25.5

# ==============================================================================
# -Dtrust_module=enabled, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE HERE
# ==============================================================================
# The option defaults to 'auto', and meson.build:425 builds the trust module
# only if TWO things are found:
#
#     libtasn1   = dependency('libtasn1', required: get_option('trust_module'))
#     asn1Parser = find_program('asn1Parser', required: get_option('trust_module'))
#
# The second is a PROGRAM, not a library -- libtasn1 ships /usr/bin/asn1Parser
# alongside the .so, and p11-kit executes it at build time. Under 'auto'
# neither is required, so a build that could not find either produces a
# complete, installable p11-kit WITH NO TRUST MODULE: no CA anchors, and a
# GnuTLS above it that validates certificates against nothing. There is no
# error and no warning; the module simply is not built.
#
# Pinned 'enabled' so both become hard requirements. pkgs/libtasn1/install.sh
# asserts asn1Parser from the producing side, because a DT_NEEDED scan cannot
# see an exec-time dependency.
#
# ==============================================================================
# -Dtrust_paths, WHICH THE BOOK CANNOT ANSWER FOR US
# ==============================================================================
# BLFS passes -Dtrust_paths=/etc/pki/anchors, because BLFS installs make-ca,
# which creates that directory as a pile of individual anchor files. THIS
# DISTRIBUTION HAS NO SUCH DIRECTORY. Read out of the published package rather
# than assumed -- ca-certificates ships exactly three files:
#
#     /etc/pki/tls/certs/ca-bundle.crt
#     /etc/ssl/cert.pem
#     /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
#
# all of them PEM bundles. Copying the book's path verbatim would compile in a
# TRUST_PATHS pointing at a directory that never exists, and the trust module
# would load zero anchors -- present, correct, and empty, which is the failure
# this whole file is written to avoid.
#
# /etc/pki/tls/certs/ca-bundle.crt is the one named: p11-kit accepts a bundle
# file as well as a directory, and this is the path ca-certificates ships that
# sits under the same /etc/pki tree the book's default assumes.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE REST
# ==============================================================================
# -Dsystemd=disabled. THE DEFAULT WOULD ALSO HAVE BEEN CORRECT, and this is
# named because it would have been correct BY ACCIDENT. meson.build:445-447 asks
# for libsystemd AND systemd and gates socket activation on both being found;
# elogind provides a libsystemd.pc symlink but no systemd.pc, so the conjunction
# fails closed and the feature stays off. That is the right answer arrived at
# through a compatibility symlink and a two-term AND -- state it instead.
#
# -Dlibffi=enabled. 'auto', and libffi IS packaged so it resolves on today.
# Named because libffi is what builds the closures p11-kit uses to wrap
# modules; without it the build falls back to a fixed pool of precompiled
# closures (-Dclosures, default 64) and silently starts failing once more than
# that many are needed.
#
# -Dbash_completion=disabled. 'auto', and bash IS packaged, so it would install
# completions into a directory nothing on a running Duct system reads.
#
# -Dtest=false. THE UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS TRUE. It only builds test programs --
# nothing is installed either way -- but it is build time spent on binaries that
# cannot be run, since _scripts/build-meson.sh never invokes meson test.
#
# -Dnls=true is already the default and gettext is packaged.
# -Dman=false and -Dgtk_doc=false are already the defaults; both want tools
# that are not packaged.
MESON_ARGS="-Dtrust_module=enabled -Dtrust_paths=/etc/pki/tls/certs/ca-bundle.crt \
-Dsystemd=disabled -Dlibffi=enabled -Dbash_completion=disabled -Dtest=false"
